INT. FERRY CAFE - RAINY DAY

Steam on the windows. A chalkboard menu. Wet umbrellas in a bucket. The ferry's engine thrums through the floor.

HAZEL OKONKWO-LARSEN, 30s, hair damp, sits at a corner table with a cup of tea. Across from her, JONAH PELLEGRINI, 30s, in a yellow raincoat, stirs sugar into coffee. Next to them, a stranger, WENDELL ACHTERBERG, 60s, reads a newspaper pretending not to listen.

HAZEL
You said you'd be on the nine o'clock.

JONAH
I missed it.

HAZEL
You always miss it.

JONAH
I got caught up.

HAZEL
In what?

JONAH
Life.

WENDELL
(from behind the paper)
That's usually what it is.

HAZEL
(turning)
Excuse me?

WENDELL
Nothing. Carry on.

JONAH
Sir, this is a private conversation.

WENDELL
It's a ferry cafe. Nothing's private.

HAZEL
He has a point.

JONAH
Hazel.

HAZEL
He does.

JONAH
You're on his side now?

WENDELL
I'm not on anyone's side. I'm reading about a cricket match.

HAZEL
(to Wendell)
Who's winning?

WENDELL
Nobody. It rained.

Jonah laughs, despite himself. Hazel's mouth twitches. The ferry horn blasts. All three look up. Rain streams down the glass.

JONAH
(softly, to Hazel)
I'm sorry.

HAZEL
(softly)
I know.

WENDELL
(lowering the paper)
And that's that.
